  "executive_briefing": "The corporate tax and international trade landscape in mid-2026 is defined by the practical execution of complex multilateral frameworks alongside targeted domestic adaptations. As the OECD's Pillar Two global minimum tax moves from negotiation to active national implementation, multinational enterprises are adjusting to a baseline 15% rate. Concurrently, ongoing tariff disputes and trade policy volatility pressure global supply chains, forcing businesses to rely on advanced data modeling to navigate cross-border trade friction. In response, regional hubs like Singapore are actively preserving their tax competitiveness. Singapore maintains its 17% flat corporate tax rate and single-tier system, supported by targeted corporate income tax rebates for the 2026 Year of Assessment, while accelerating compliance through its structured, phased GST InvoiceNow e-invoicing mandate running through 2031. Globally, the adoption of Continuous Transaction Controls (CTC) is standardizing digital transaction pipelines to close structural tax gaps.",
